#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/id #uuid "62d73be9-5f67-5f44-a45a-f5c25a4dfe27" :question/math-academy-id "e-64" :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n${6e^{x - 1}\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 1 \\\\ x^{2} + 2x + 3\\end{vmatrix}forx > 1$." :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<1$, so we use the expression\n\n$$\nf(x) = 6e^{x-1}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{-})6e^{x - 1} \\\\\n&= 6e^{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= 6e^{0} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>1$, so we use the expression\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2+2x+3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})x^{2} + 2x + 3 \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2(1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/id #uuid "e9a033d1-eb3f-5e7e-8237-346b03681499" :question/math-academy-id "e-65" :question/problem "Find the left-sided and right-sided limits at $x=0$ for the function\n$f(x) = {-x^{2} + 3\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix}forx \\ge 0$." :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0$, so we use the expression\n\n$$\nf(x)=-x^2+3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{-})(-x^{2} + 3) \\\\\n&= - (0)^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0$, so we use the expression\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})\\sqrt{x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "7f534444-765b-5c3a-a381-883a48911700" :question/math-academy-id "e-66" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x)$ for the function given by\n${\\frac{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 5}}{3x - 7}\\begin{vmatrix}forx \\le 1 \\\\ (x - 2)^{2} - 1\\end{vmatrix}forx \\ge 2$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} g(x)$, we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the right-sided limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1^+}g(x)$ does not exist since $g(x)$ is not defined in the interval $x\\in(1,2)$.\n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/id #uuid "baed0974-c209-5e29-99b5-d7f1d55b296d" :question/math-academy-id "e-67" :question/problem "For the piecewise function\n$f(x) = {4x^{3} - 2\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 1 \\\\ 3x + b\\end{vmatrix}forx > 1$,\nit is known that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of the constant $b$?" :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 1$, so we use the expression $f(x)=4x^3-2$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})4x^{3} - 2; = 4(1)^{3} - 2; = 2$.\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1$, so we use the expression $f(x)= 3x+b$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})3x + b; = 3(1) + b; = 3 + b$.\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x) \\\\\n2 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6eb97f3-da1e-583f-9beb-b714c2d594e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6eb97f3-da1e-583f-9beb-b714c2d594e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251143} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b55d56d-946c-5ba5-807c-4eecbe5277fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251142} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911cc444-eb62-53f8-8e67-c3f3eed3ae36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "911cc444-eb62-53f8-8e67-c3f3eed3ae36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251145} :answer-field/id #uuid "da12a107-aa17-5bc1-b6e0-ec22b4a7f8b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251144}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94f1b338-29da-5fa4-8842-fa97970f2c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-302142" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-3x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-3\\le x<1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-bx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=4.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $4,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $4.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $-3\\le x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}-3x+2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =a({1}^{2})-3(1)+2 \\\\  & =a-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ a-1 & =4 \\\\ a & =4+1 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $1\\le x\\le 3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-bx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}-bx) \\\\  & =3({1}^{2})-b(1) \\\\  & =3-b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ 3-b & =4 \\\\ b & =3-4 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045fffb4-9e06-586d-a853-b10378d20892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251148} {:answer/id #uuid "644df85c-5f72-56e0-9df5-abfdebd72356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644df85c-5f72-56e0-9df5-abfdebd72356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251149} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd6a0b7-1b27-5274-96ab-a1926974f446" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419fa797-bf64-5c85-911b-3a89ca338639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419fa797-bf64-5c85-911b-3a89ca338639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251151} :answer-field/id #uuid "5248d949-86fa-568c-b5a4-a781e1aca325" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d94bc837-284e-5745-a87c-0eafdefad454" :question/math-academy-id "q-339732" :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ {e}^{2+x}+3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-6(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge -2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{2+x}+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{e}^{2+x}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2+(-2)}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1506879a-40a4-5588-92da-c27bcf122f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1506879a-40a4-5588-92da-c27bcf122f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251154} :answer-field/id #uuid "513a33ea-c554-5106-ba89-32723b991fae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d540969a-8204-54d6-b275-8a37ed4ce9bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339892" :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=8$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>8,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b0c3ace-7a61-54fe-8c76-7fb177851cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251157} {:answer/id #uuid "fb20f698-ecbd-5cc9-9607-91a51f6a0268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b0c3ace-7a61-54fe-8c76-7fb177851cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251157} :answer-field/id #uuid "304e0016-edb4-5b3d-90f9-e2d2f8e923e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62edf372-39a6-5213-8992-606ee7160c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62edf372-39a6-5213-8992-606ee7160c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251160} :answer-field/id #uuid "14759065-5e95-5216-b43f-1d85af5ea2bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251159}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f147a66-27be-5b9c-9c82-1dfc6dc18520" :question/math-academy-id "q-339939" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (-1+1))-5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-2}+(2-2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+{0}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b90ee35a-4717-5a42-a548-0b5f77e31cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90ee35a-4717-5a42-a548-0b5f77e31cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251163} :answer-field/id #uuid "70bc4c13-8f74-5e8d-8f95-8291d34c577d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232a2351-9efd-573f-901f-5043ce47b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232a2351-9efd-573f-901f-5043ce47b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b61a3d-3272-539b-a33b-053f8faa6297" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa3be5bb-98bd-5c95-a4d6-b0df3476c988" :question/math-academy-id "q-339967" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}[3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2] \\\\  & =3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0))+2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{7x+2}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{7x+2}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7(2)+2}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e8ca14-763b-5506-954f-eb3561049fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e8ca14-763b-5506-954f-eb3561049fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251168} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6afa44-4807-5dbb-8586-a1a918d4517b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a5d834-2e20-5b4d-963a-94cc03176bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a5d834-2e20-5b4d-963a-94cc03176bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251170} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0c96fe-89a3-5834-ac24-3728fbd2f0a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3afc958b-0814-5f07-86e8-35c3521bb224" :question/math-academy-id "q-340040" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}+2x-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ 4x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=9.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $8.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x\\le 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+2x-3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =a({2}^{2})+2(2)-3 \\\\  & =4a+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 4a+1 & =9 \\\\ 4a & =8 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(4x+b) \\\\  & =4(2)+b \\\\  & =8+b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 8+b & =9 \\\\ b & =9-8 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ae7f5e-bf9b-512b-8be2-f1734af5a16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251173} {:answer/id #uuid "b92db5cd-0671-5e46-9d45-b7a1dd7dda9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84ae7f5e-bf9b-512b-8be2-f1734af5a16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251173} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a635c6-0080-5232-986d-e9e04906e397" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251172}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b91dcbc6-c425-5994-85d5-426529628a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-340301" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ p{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=3.$ What is the value of the constant $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $3,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $3.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{e}^{\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(1-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(0)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=p{x}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(p{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =p\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =p\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "762a6ab8-dd63-580d-b922-46761c15d5bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251177} {:answer/id #uuid "2c23969f-21d6-59fa-ae3a-12250146c743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251178} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d60a80-1a19-5e81-b835-7075f2bcf3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251179} {:answer/id #uuid "a48c4ec8-a20d-5ff2-afdb-ec2dd23b8b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251180} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8314ce-a943-5add-a8e6-be8a969fa8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a48c4ec8-a20d-5ff2-afdb-ec2dd23b8b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251180} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4860b19-76cc-5025-ac54-fb11d1481f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c632534-5515-5827-b9ee-67d121a8015a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36689" :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\sqrt{-x}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8d901ab-15ab-57e7-8c7c-cd8e3f466fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251184} {:answer/id #uuid "67892502-55d0-5b71-b8b4-668fea14cc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251185} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49614-ecc1-5d6a-a7f4-888e080bd9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251186} {:answer/id #uuid "2498c86d-1c86-566a-b246-c7af422be22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251187} {:answer/id #uuid "b77b1d51-b805-522e-ba12-262953b5224b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49614-ecc1-5d6a-a7f4-888e080bd9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251186} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ec17f2-c83b-5128-bf98-03e78b8f190d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251183}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65911f9a-fa00-54b8-9673-9586456d002e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c5e59b-bd3a-5df8-a9a6-69f7eb6a518f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103824} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cdf6a5-2131-57ee-b209-3b0a2aa7a93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103825} {:answer/id #uuid "ebcb5e6b-81df-52c2-b346-e325771c7d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103826} {:answer/id #uuid "05b9a07f-a3fa-55ef-a70e-9cdfa6980358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) =-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103827} {:answer/id #uuid "18a8a0b5-54a7-50d3-95bd-1a3bb5dc0dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4cdf6a5-2131-57ee-b209-3b0a2aa7a93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103825} :answer-field/id #uuid "3493c78b-2db6-53a0-a112-4b0ca6e94b0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f374e70-63fe-5c69-b0b8-cea5fdbc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-36702" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x = 2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n$f(x) = {x^{2} - x - 2\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 2 \\\\ -x^{2} + 6x - 8\\end{vmatrix}forx \\ge 2$." :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-x-2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}{x}^{2}-x-2 \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-2-2 \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e5efc3-6d95-5a3b-bb0a-fbe1793da354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103836} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf9b025-ca25-5b26-81b0-acced984a77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103837} {:answer/id #uuid "9303c483-7bef-56b0-8dbf-598237c3dc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103838} {:answer/id #uuid "db759a3b-c380-5027-8d6b-f4402e3808c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103839} {:answer/id #uuid "fda1a274-15d1-571f-88c0-477ae2a852c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9303c483-7bef-56b0-8dbf-598237c3dc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103838} :answer-field/id #uuid "2507f0bd-fe50-5029-880d-0c1bd7c8b661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "879a03e2-86ea-5ccb-9d0a-3118319f3b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-36707" :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x = 2$ for the function\n$f(x) = {x^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}forx \\le 2 \\\\ x\\end{vmatrix}forx > 2$." :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}x \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb328d7b-ad8d-533c-bc71-f0c0da871646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251191} {:answer/id #uuid "ad775e72-87f7-54a1-91d0-d59a27a12e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251192} {:answer/id #uuid "b348ba9d-a7ad-5471-ba58-11703cd47f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251193} {:answer/id #uuid "7e60bf61-e857-5b0b-8df3-21f1fa5b18c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251194} {:answer/id #uuid "72c8747c-b211-5c74-8503-331dab0f0241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b348ba9d-a7ad-5471-ba58-11703cd47f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251193} :answer-field/id #uuid "277453aa-cef8-5ae2-aa8d-700d172e8830" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251190}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8e54158-9b0b-5467-b616-e74e1922dae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-36712" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}x \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a193b055-26f2-5fa7-81ea-f9d494715f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251198} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dcc2c8-07db-5c23-8ff7-34f4c000ffa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251199} {:answer/id #uuid "938764f7-b8ee-58fa-a2b2-c02af0672079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251200} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca728f2-222f-5999-ae9e-98fcc6a6a393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251201} {:answer/id #uuid "c784541e-c62f-5d95-9a7e-66e2f4a91957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "938764f7-b8ee-58fa-a2b2-c02af0672079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251200} :answer-field/id #uuid "32251415-ec0f-5079-bdb4-a2aedb7c2af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4da9acce-fbe4-5910-b619-3be32acf4b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-36720" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6x+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4{log}_{2}⁡x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390bec18-e583-5fa1-bcc0-13d5ad2c6cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251205} {:answer/id #uuid "b9551551-e593-5800-8bcc-59d2098a3e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251206} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab3f48c-bee3-5016-9a2b-8ab201cb770d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251207} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9ecb6d-cfb8-5e87-bddd-6e44e77f6b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251208} {:answer/id #uuid "8096a8e3-284f-5d79-8411-40ba3aac3e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab3f48c-bee3-5016-9a2b-8ab201cb770d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251207} :answer-field/id #uuid "af298a4f-2c6a-5364-b205-4cf2b8146e7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "788e4f94-c59e-54a9-87d5-7621f4a91cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-36724" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fd240ab-66b3-5a3f-ad0e-294897bff253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251212} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f59ba2-aec7-5662-bebf-3aee6a573d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251213} {:answer/id #uuid "dd01d823-189f-5583-bcf8-fc8ecb07ac33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251214} {:answer/id #uuid "dacafd47-ded0-58ea-ad60-6fe5895e5066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251215} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d24a12-464b-52da-8288-0fe36930e287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fd240ab-66b3-5a3f-ad0e-294897bff253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251212} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79e76bc-e745-5d75-996e-ed5cbd310fe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251211}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7c4e097-3c68-515f-a652-a3cde28c0e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36883" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=4$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,0\\le x<4 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ for }\\,x=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\text{ for }\\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e286f49-4122-52df-aff2-2a3460380287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251219} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d91ad-a490-594e-98e7-599620d66a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251220} {:answer/id #uuid "484114ef-32e4-514d-85f9-fe3452f2c29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251221} {:answer/id #uuid "61db10cc-da85-527c-a90e-3e9220155ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251222} {:answer/id #uuid "a8804906-47c5-51b5-93dc-75941a380720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8804906-47c5-51b5-93dc-75941a380720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251223} :answer-field/id #uuid "31739614-c263-5d8d-a97f-dd0fe4e459df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251218}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d10373-1baf-5303-9f9f-7dbe595ac72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37488" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c05a92f-9232-5a57-8d62-60cd2794ef0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251226} {:answer/id #uuid "2639cb3c-6fa9-56a9-8800-dfade5e09150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251227} {:answer/id #uuid "5061f948-f802-5f85-bda6-c0b634f3fbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251228} {:answer/id #uuid "8b68b77b-2add-5ab1-84b2-acee5015d68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251229} {:answer/id #uuid "9f84b58c-2a20-539e-9ad3-de7114007287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5061f948-f802-5f85-bda6-c0b634f3fbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251228} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c8b20a-17e9-51f7-9df8-a61ec673f47c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251225}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "440fe138-4ebc-525f-9943-9ea9999a6962" :question/math-academy-id "q-99959" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=-1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ -2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=-1 \\\\ x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5f167b-39aa-5aa8-958c-fb5b97f70240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251233} {:answer/id #uuid "de06d3a4-f52d-5112-842e-854d7528e11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251234} {:answer/id #uuid "0088c894-6d66-58d9-a277-1f166377306c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251235} {:answer/id #uuid "d9438753-96a1-520c-b7ef-053b52acf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251236} {:answer/id #uuid "301ca70d-9d1e-5e85-ab1e-1861d2e619d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de06d3a4-f52d-5112-842e-854d7528e11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251234} :answer-field/id #uuid "b254be2b-ffa7-5487-ae80-d26c76875486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251232}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "864478b8-5b0e-578f-950b-2e87704cfe73" :question/math-academy-id "q-99961" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+c) \\\\  & =-1+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 1 & =-1+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247e7d11-197b-56d1-ae3c-3574eebd2658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251240} {:answer/id #uuid "afd82805-3abb-589c-9fa4-8c52227a70d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251241} {:answer/id #uuid "5c979e1f-f47e-5a2a-a836-8f220fe112b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251242} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1d8a3d-fa87-54fe-a67d-9801a785f5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251243} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc07858-2299-52f7-97ab-3d2cea5d86bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fc07858-2299-52f7-97ab-3d2cea5d86bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251244} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b43d5a5-8899-5fe9-af75-1f4ec1e718c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251239}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59f2b360-e6a9-5abb-b31f-45e821295526" :question/math-academy-id "q-99967" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ x & \\text{ for }\\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the left-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8915084-d164-5cb7-888a-acc52832840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251247} {:answer/id #uuid "be42a67f-09f8-5c88-8c91-a2ce482097d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251248} {:answer/id #uuid "b8aa663f-b2d6-58fc-bb78-83fff5a053f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251249} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7af7bb-d845-5c47-80ab-58561f811e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251250} {:answer/id #uuid "71edf051-9332-52d4-b871-fc764f13606f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8915084-d164-5cb7-888a-acc52832840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251247} :answer-field/id #uuid "01af7915-2566-5bce-a672-a4d1f0cc908d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbf774b1-1097-516c-b299-98eaeb5e8067" :question/math-academy-id "q-99968" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
